.TH GGE\-MATH 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-math \- vectors, interpolation, collision tests, and random numbers
.SH DESCRIPTION
The math module is three headers of small \fBstatic inline\fR functions: mathx.h (Vec2 and scalar helpers), collide.h (rectangle and circle tests), rng.h (a seedable random generator). Nothing here allocates or keeps state except the \fBuint64_t\fR you hand the RNG.
.PP
.SH VECTORS (mathx.h)
\fBVec2\fR is {float x, y}. Everything takes and returns values:
.TP
\fBv2(x, y)\fR
build one.
.TP
\fBv2_add(a, b), v2_sub(a, b)\fR
sum; difference (the vector from b to a).
.TP
\fBv2_scale(a, s)\fR
multiply by a scalar.
.TP
\fBv2_len(a), v2_dist(a, b)\fR
length; distance between points.
.TP
\fBv2_norm(a)\fR
unit length, or zero if a is zero (no division by zero).
.TP
\fBv2_dot(a, b)\fR
dot product; for unit vectors the cosine of the angle between them.
.TP
\fBv2_lerp(a, b, t)\fR
a at t = 0, b at t = 1.
.SS Motion
Velocity is units per second; a tick moves by \fBvel * dt\fR:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
p\->pos = v2_add(p\->pos, v2_scale(p\->vel, dt));
.EE
.RE
.PP
Acceleration: \fBvel = v2_add(vel, v2_scale(accel, dt))\fR before the position update. Gravity is an acceleration of (0, +G). Friction: \fBvel = v2_scale(vel, powf(0.001f, dt))\fR decays to a thousandth per second at any tick rate (or use \fBsmooth_t\fR).
.PP
.SS Directions and aiming
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
Vec2 to_target = v2_sub(target, pos);           /* points at the target */
Vec2 dir = v2_norm(to_target);                  /* unit direction */
float dist = v2_len(to_target);
bullet.vel = v2_scale(dir, 300);                /* 300 px/s toward it */
float angle_deg = atan2f(dir.y, dir.x) * 180.0f / 3.14159265f;   /* for draw_texture_ex / sprite_draw */
Vec2 from_angle = v2(cosf(a), sinf(a));         /* a in radians */
bool in_front = v2_dot(facing, dir) > 0.5f;     /* within ~60 degrees of facing (both unit) */
.EE
.RE
.PP
Screen y grows downward, so an angle of 90 degrees points down and "up" is negative y.
.PP
.SS Scalars
.TP
\fBlerpf(a, b, t)\fR
linear interpolation of floats.
.TP
\fBclampf(v, lo, hi), clampi(v, lo, hi)\fR
clamp.
.TP
\fBminf, maxf\fR
min and max.
.TP
\fBsignf(v)\fR
\-1, 0 or 1.
.TP
\fBsmooth_t(smoothing, dt)\fR
frame\-rate\-independent fraction to move toward a target: \fBx = lerpf(x, target, smooth_t(0.2f, dt))\fR closes about 63% of the gap every 0.2 s regardless of tick rate. camera_follow(3) is this.
.SH COLLISION TESTS (collide.h)
.TP
\fBrect_at(pos, w, h), rect_centered(c, w, h), rect_center(r)\fR
build and inspect hitboxes (gge\-types(7)).
.TP
\fBrect_overlaps(a, b)\fR
two rectangles share area (touching edges do not count).
.TP
\fBrect_contains(r, p)\fR
a point is inside.
.TP
\fBcircle_overlaps(a, ra, b, rb)\fR
two circles overlap; no square root.
.TP
\fBrect_hit_side(a, b)\fR
for overlapping a and b, which side of b a came from: HIT_TOP (a is above), HIT_BOTTOM, HIT_LEFT, HIT_RIGHT.
.SS Using them
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
/* player vs enemy: stomp or take damage */
Rect pr = player_hitbox(&g\->player), er = enemy_hitbox(en);
if (rect_overlaps(pr, er)) {
  if (rect_hit_side(pr, er) == HIT_TOP && g\->player.vel.y > 0) { en\->alive = false; g\->player.vel.y = \-150; }
  else if (g\->player.invuln <= 0) { g\->player.hp\-\-; g\->player.invuln = 1.0f; }
}
/* ball vs paddle */
if (rect_overlaps(ball_r, paddle_r)) {
  HitSide s = rect_hit_side(ball_r, paddle_r);
  if (s == HIT_LEFT || s == HIT_RIGHT) g\->ball.vel.x = \-g\->ball.vel.x; else g\->ball.vel.y = \-g\->ball.vel.y;
}
/* pickup radius */
if (circle_overlaps(g\->player.pos, 10, coin\->pos, 6)) coin\->alive = false;
/* mouse over a button */
if (rect_contains(button, v2(in\->mouse_x, in\->mouse_y))) ...
.EE
.RE
.PP
For entities against solid tiles use tilemap_move(3), which resolves each axis and reports \fBon_ground\fR. For two moving boxes that must not interpenetrate (pushing crates), move one axis at a time and use rect_hit_side to push out along the shallow axis.
.PP
.SS Shapes the tests do not cover
Rotated rectangles, polygons and lines against boxes are not provided. Approximate with a circle (most things), several small circles along a line (a sword swing), or a point test on the tip. A ray against tiles is a loop of tilemap_solid_at(3) samples every few pixels along the ray.
.PP
.SH RANDOM NUMBERS (rng.h)
The generator is a \fBuint64_t\fR state you own, normally \fBGameState.rng\fR, seeded once at new game. Every call takes its address and advances it.
.TP
\fBrng_next(s)\fR
raw 64\-bit value (rarely needed).
.TP
\fBrng_float(s)\fR
[0, 1).
.TP
\fBrng_range(s, lo, hi)\fR
integer in [lo, hi] inclusive; \fBrng_range(&g\->rng, 1, 6)\fR is a die.
.TP
\fBrng_between(s, lo, hi)\fR
float in [lo, hi).
.TP
\fBrng_chance(s, p)\fR
true with probability p.
.SS Determinism
Same seed, same sequence of calls, same results, on every machine. That is what makes a save file replay, a daily\-challenge seed shareable, and a bug reproducible from its seed. Keep it true by rolling only from \fBupdate\fR (never from render or handle_input) and by seeding once. For repeatable testing set the seed to a constant in game_state_init.
.PP
.SS Independent streams
Content that must not depend on how many enemies rolled before it (a level layout, loot tables) uses its own temporary state derived from a stable number:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
uint64_t s = g\->world_seed ^ ((uint64_t)level * 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15ULL);
for (...) tiles[i] = rng_chance(&s, 0.3f) ? WALL : FLOOR;
.EE
.RE
.PP
.SS Weighted choice
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static const int weights[] = { 50, 30, 15, 5 };   /* common .. legendary */
int total = 100, r = rng_range(&g\->rng, 1, total), pick = 0;
while (r > weights[pick]) r \-= weights[pick++];
.EE
.RE
.PP
.SS Shuffle (a deck of cards)
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
for (int i = n \- 1; i > 0; \-\-i) { int j = rng_range(&g\->rng, 0, i); int t = deck[i]; deck[i] = deck[j]; deck[j] = t; }
.EE
.RE
.PP
.SH NUMERIC HABITS
.IP \(bu 2
Use \fBfloat\fR everywhere; \fBsqrtf\fR, \fBfloorf\fR, \fBpowf\fR, \fBsinf\fR (the f versions) from math.h.
.IP \(bu 2
Compare squared distances when only ordering matters: \fBv2_dot(d, d) < r * r\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Positions accumulate float error slowly; snapping to whole pixels only at draw time is fine.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB(int)floorf(x / 16)\fR for tile coordinates; a plain cast rounds toward zero and misplaces negative x.
